# Master Engineering Prompt
# Universal Interactive Android Runtime Explorer & Controller

## 1. Project Vision

Design and implement a professional, modular, extensible Android Runtime Exploration and Control Platform.

The project must be delivered as a reusable Android library ecosystem, not merely a standalone debugging application.

Its primary purpose is to give an authorized user a unified interface to inspect, navigate, interact with, and control accessible runtime components of an Android process.

This is NOT just a monitoring or information-gathering tool.

The user must be able to:

- Discover classes, objects, services, Binder interfaces, and runtime components.
- Navigate through classes and object instances.
- Inspect fields, properties, methods, constructors, and relationships.
- Read and modify accessible object fields.
- Invoke methods and constructors.
- Explore method parameters and construct their input values interactively.
- Execute methods using a dedicated Runtime Runner.
- Inspect execution results, exceptions, and side effects.
- Navigate from one object to another through references.
- Explore system services and Binder endpoints.
- Perform supported runtime operations through one integrated interface.

The overall experience should resemble a combination of:

- Runtime Explorer
- Object Browser
- Reflection Inspector
- Interactive Debugger
- Binder Explorer
- Runtime Method Executor
- System Service Explorer
- Runtime Control Studio

All these capabilities must be integrated into ONE unified Explorer.

Reflection must NOT be implemented as a separate standalone feature or disconnected screen. It must be the underlying interaction engine of the Explorer.

---

# 2. Dual-Environment Architecture

The platform must support two execution environments.

## Environment A: Application Runtime

A reusable library integrated into an ordinary Android application.

Requirements:

- Discover classes loaded in the host process where supported.
- Inspect accessible objects registered with the Explorer.
- Inspect class hierarchies, interfaces, methods, and fields.
- Navigate object references.
- Read and modify accessible fields.
- Invoke accessible methods.
- Create objects using supported constructors.
- Explore application services and components.
- Inspect available Binder proxies and interfaces.
- Execute methods through the Runtime Runner.

The library must work without requiring a separate desktop application for normal operation.

## Environment B: System Runtime

A dedicated integration that can operate inside an authorized Android platform environment.

Target environments include:

- system_server
- Android Framework services
- SystemService implementations
- LocalServices
- System process components
- Other explicitly integrated platform processes

Requirements:

- Discover system runtime classes and instances.
- Inspect internal Framework objects where access is available.
- Explore system services.
- Explore LocalServices registrations.
- Inspect Binder services and their accessible interfaces.
- Invoke supported methods on authorized objects.
- Modify supported fields.
- Execute controlled operations inside the integrated process.
- Support platform-specific APIs and internal Android classes.

Important architectural requirement:

Do not assume that an ordinary Android AAR can independently access system_server.

Implement a separate Platform Integration layer using the appropriate AOSP/platform build configuration, permissions, and process integration.

The shared Explorer UI and core engine should communicate with the platform integration through a well-defined provider/agent interface.

## Multi-Process Architecture

The architecture must support multiple runtime agents.

For example:

- Application Process Agent
- system_server Agent
- Other authorized Process Agents

Each agent must maintain its own runtime context.

Use stable object identifiers and handles instead of attempting to transfer Java object references between processes.

Communication must use a suitable IPC abstraction.

