# 3. Core Architectural Modules

Design the following modules as independently testable components.

### 3.1 Runtime Core

Responsibilities:

- Runtime initialization
- Environment detection
- Process identification
- API-level detection
- Runtime capability detection
- Runtime session management
- Agent registration
- Provider management
- Lifecycle management

### 3.2 Class Discovery Engine

Discover and organize available class information.

Capabilities:

- Loaded class discovery where supported.
- ClassLoader discovery.
- DEX metadata discovery.
- Package and namespace filtering.
- Class hierarchy inspection.
- Interface discovery.
- Superclass inspection.
- Annotation inspection.
- Method metadata.
- Field metadata.
- Constructor metadata.
- Class origin information.
- ClassLoader association.

Clearly distinguish:

1. Classes discovered in DEX files.
2. Classes actually loaded by the runtime.
3. Classes for which an instance is available.

Never represent these three categories as equivalent.

### 3.3 Object Registry

This is one of the most important components.

The Explorer must support discovering, registering, tracking, and inspecting object references obtained through supported providers.

Capabilities:

- Object registration.
- Object identification.
- Object metadata.
- Object type information.
- Object ownership and process association.
- Reference navigation.
- Object relationship tracking.
- Object lifecycle tracking where possible.
- Object search.
- Object filtering.
- Object grouping.
- Object history.

Use stable Object IDs or handles.

Do not claim that ordinary Java reflection can enumerate every object in the process heap.

Where deeper heap inspection is available through an authorized JVMTI or platform integration, implement it as an optional capability provider.

### 3.4 Class Inspector

When a class is selected, show:

- Class name.
- Package.
- Modifiers.
- Superclass.
- Implemented interfaces.
- ClassLoader.
- Fields.
- Methods.
- Constructors.
- Annotations.
- Static members.
- Instance member definitions.
- Inheritance hierarchy.

Allow navigation into related classes.

### 3.5 Object Inspector

When an object is selected, display:

- Object ID.
- Runtime class.
- Identity information.
- Object state.
- Available fields.
- Current field values.
- Available methods.
- Related objects.
- Reference relationships.
- Object hierarchy.

The user must be able to expand an object and navigate into its fields and referenced objects.

Example:

Object A
  ├── field: user
  │     └── Object B
  │           ├── name
  │           ├── age
  │           └── getProfile()
  │
  ├── field: repository
  │     └── Object C
  │           ├── database
  │           └── loadData()
  │
  └── refresh()

This navigation must be interactive and recursive.

Support configurable depth limits and cycle detection to prevent infinite object graph traversal.

### 3.6 Reflection Engine

Reflection must be embedded into the Explorer architecture.

It must provide:

- Field discovery.
- Field reading.
- Supported field modification.
- Method discovery.
- Method invocation.
- Constructor discovery.
- Constructor invocation.
- Method overload resolution.
- Parameter metadata.
- Return type information.
- Exception handling.
- Static and instance member handling.
- Inheritance-aware member inspection.
- Access capability reporting.

Support public APIs and additional authorized integration providers for internal APIs.

Do not silently bypass Android security restrictions.

When an operation is unavailable, explain why and identify the required integration or capability.

