# 17. API Design

Expose a clean public SDK.

Example conceptual API:

```kotlin
val explorer = RuntimeExplorer.initialize(
    context = context,
    configuration = ExplorerConfiguration(
        enableClassDiscovery = true,
        enableObjectRegistry = true,
        enableReflection = true,
        enableMethodExecution = true,
        enableBinderExplorer = true
    )
)

explorer.start()

val session = explorer.currentSession()

val classes = session.classes.search("UserManager")

val objects = session.objects.findByType("UserManager")

val objectInspector = session.inspect(objects.first())

val fields = objectInspector.fields()

val methods = objectInspector.methods()

val result = session.runner.execute(
    target = objects.first(),
    method = "someMethod",
    arguments = listOf(/* resolved arguments */)
)
```

This is conceptual API design.

Implement the real API according to the final architecture, type system, and provider capabilities.

The public SDK must provide stable interfaces without exposing unnecessary implementation details.

